able_reason":"The item asks for evidence records in the trace (loop.jsonl or walkthrough), which the orchestrator writes from its own runs. No code behaviour changes, so no test in src/ or tests/ can fail before or pass after. Closure is checked by the reviewer reading the receipt, and the TrxReport part needs a scope decision first."},{"id":"I6","rule_id":"none","layer":"tests","files":["Directory.Build.props"],"root_cause":"Layer note: the schema has no build-config value, so 'tests' is used here. The defect is in shared build configuration for all four src projects, and the guard test belongs in Architecture.Tests. Symptom: if BannedSymbols.txt were deleted, RS0030 would silently stop applying, and the comment narrates build history. Cause: this is a scaffold-era accommodation that was never removed. The scaffold referenced BannedSymbols.txt before the file existed, so the AdditionalFiles item was made conditional on Exists() (Directory.Build.props:20), and the comment explained when the file would arrive (Directory.Build.props:16). The file landed in services green (cd0ea2b, loop.jsonl:14). The refactor step (fe93e5b, loop.jsonl:17) changed only one whitespace line in Persistence, so the guard and the history comment survived. Both the comment and the guard trace to this one cause and are not split. This breaks the architecture contract's 'RS0030 as an error, in every src project' and the slop rule against conditions no case needs. Fix direction: make the BannedSymbols.txt item unconditional for every src project, so a missing file fails the build. Rewrite the comment to say why the item exists, not when it arrived.","fingerprint":"none|Directory.Build.props|bannedsymbols-exists-guard","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given Directory.Build.props at the repository root When the AdditionalFiles item that includes BannedSymbols.txt for non-test projects is inspected Then its ItemGroup condition contains no Exists() clause, so RS0030 cannot be disabled by a missing