+{"schema_version":1,"plan_id":"build","node_id":"F0b","attempt":1,"source":"david","source_ref":"loop.jsonl steer(D1,pause1) at 261a87f: change: add launchSettings for 5080","findings":[{"id":"I1","rule_id":"none (HITL port 5080, docs/requirements.md:156; architecture tree Properties/launchSettings.json (port 5080))","layer":"api","files":["src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Properties/launchSettings.json","docs/plans/active/build.dag.yaml"],"root_cause":"Symptom: pressing F5 in VS 2026 does not start the API on http://localhost:5080, which is the port that tests/postman/local.postman_environment.json:6 targets (baseUrl http://localhost:5080). Cause: the scaffold never produced a committed launch profile. The architecture contract (.claude/skills/architecture/SKILL.md:30) lists Properties/launchSettings.json (port 5080) under the Api project, and docs/requirements.md:156 says David runs the API from the worktree on port 5080 with the .slnx open in VS 2026. But the t1 scaffold task (docs/plans/active/build.dag.yaml:90) only lists the slnx, global.json, the props files and the csproj files, so launchSettings was left out, and nothing at HEAD 261a87f tracks it. The worktree does contain an untracked src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Properties/launchSettings.json (git status shows ?? src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Properties/). No agent write to it appears in docs/trace/build/F0b/activity.jsonl, and its single profile at line 9 binds random ports, https://localhost:49876;http://localhost:49877. That pattern is consistent with VS generating the file when the Web SDK project (src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Uinsure.Policies.Api.csproj:1) was opened without one. That generated file, not a missing port flag, is why F5 lands on 49876/49877. A secondary point: it puts https first, but Postman uses plain http and Program.cs:1-13 has no HTTPS endpoint configuration or redirection, so an https-first profile also fails to match the HITL contract. Fix direction: replace the VS-generated file with a committed launchSettings.json. It should have one project profile whose only applicationUrl is http://localhost:5080 (no https listener and no random ports), and ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT set to Development, so that F5, `dotnet run` and the Postman local environment all agree. If a browser is launched, it should open a route that exists in F0b (for example /health/live) rather than the root, which has no route. Leave out machine-specific values (no absolute paths or user names) so the pre-commit privacy scan passes. The CLI command in CLAUDE.md:60 still works, because --urls overrides the profile's applicationUrl. Commit it as a loop change on feature/build/F0b with the standard trailers. Optionally, record in the dag that the scaffold or stub step owns this file, so later nodes do not regenerate it.","fingerprint":"none|src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Properties/launchSettings.json|launch-profile-missing-5080","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"launchSettings.json is read only by Visual Studio and by the `dotnet run` launch-profile loader. Api.Tests runs the app through WebApplicationFactory on an in-memory TestServer, which binds no Kestrel port and ignores launch profiles, so no test in the suite can show which port F5 binds. A test that parses the JSON and compares applicationUrl with the Postman baseUrl could catch drift between the two files, but it would check file text, not launch behaviour. Adding one is David's call. The real proof is manual: in VS 2026, F5 from the worktree, then GET http://localhost:5080/health/live returns 200 and the Postman local environment health request passes. Record that receipt in loop.jsonl at Pause 1 or Pause 2."}],"previous_attempts":["attempt 1: t1 scaffold done (Api CS5001 leg waived by David, gate.scaffold.waived at 8b59f0d); t2 red done; t3 stub done at 6e3e9fd; Pause 1 reached at 261a87f; D1 is the first steer, has no earlier fingerprint, and does not count toward the retry ladder"]}
